coder › Done. I changed `src/key.ts` and added tests in `src/key.test.ts`, then committed on `work/main`.

What changed
- `cacheKey` now runs a `withSortedKeys` pass before `JSON.stringify`:
  - Plain objects (and `Object.create(null)` objects) have their own string keys sorted with `Object.keys(...).sort()`, at every depth.
  - Arrays are mapped element-by-element, so their order is preserved while any object elements still get sorted keys.
  - Non-plain objects (like `Date`, `RegExp`, boxed primitives, `Map`) are left untouched, so `JSON.stringify` still applies its normal behavior — which is what turns a `Date` into its ISO string.
  - `undefined` and function values are passed through, so `JSON.stringify` omits them from objects (and turns them into `null` in arrays).
  - `NaN`/`Infinity` are passed through, so `JSON.stringify` turns them into `null`.
- Used a `Object.create(null)` accumulator for the sorted object so a key like `"__proto__"` is serialized correctly instead of mutating the prototype.
- For top-level `undefined`/function/symbol, where `JSON.stringify` returns `undefined`, the function falls back to `String(value)` so the declared `string` return type holds.

Tests added (in `src/key.test.ts`)
- top-level and nested key order equivalence
- array order is preserved
- object elements inside arrays are still key-sorted
- `undefined` and function values omitted from objects
- `Date` serialized as its ISO string
- `NaN`, `Infinity`, and `-Infinity` become `null`

